# Search / filter form design

When you add or change **search/filter form** UI (for example main search, activity log, statistics, search history, pending approvals, user management, or permission groups), follow the design definition docs so that field size, type, constraints, and data source stay consistent. **Standard-first**: see `.cursor/rules/ux-frontend-standard-first.mdc` and `docs/design/ux-frontend-standard-principles.md`. If a relevant standard is missing or unclear, do not implement; inform the user and ask for standard definition first.

## Mandatory references

1. **Field-level definition schema**: `docs/design/search-field-definition-items.md` (definition items: fieldId, label, controlType, width, height, constraints, dataSource, etc.)
2. **Per-screen field definitions**: `docs/design/search-fields-by-screen.md` (per-screen field definitions; statistics follows the same user and extra-block rules as activity log)
3. **Form/filter layout and rules**: `docs/design/forms-and-filters.md` (filter group title placement, compact variant, panel width, **single application point**, **width by role**)
4. **CSS standard and exceptions**: `frontend/src/styles/search-filter-standard.css` (single source; **use wrapper class** so control sizing is applied in one place). For screen-specific overrides: component CSS only, with comment and `docs/design/css-standard-and-exceptions.md` §5 Exception index. See `docs/design/css-standard-and-exceptions.md`.

## Single application point for control sizing

- **One wrapper, one rule set**: Search/filter panels (including header date range, e.g. StatisticsHeader) must use the **standard wrapper class** (e.g. `sf-compact-panel`) on the form or filter root so that control height, padding, border, and font-size are applied **only** from `search-filter-standard.css`. See `docs/design/forms-and-filters.md` § Single application point and `docs/design/css-standard-and-exceptions.md` §3.
- **Do not re-declare** in component CSS: Components must **not** duplicate `height`, `min-height`, `padding`, `border`, `border-radius`, or `font-size` for `input.form-control`, `select.form-control`, or filter buttons when those elements are inside the wrapper. Component CSS may only add **layout** (grid/flex, gaps, block structure) and **width by role** (min/max from vars or design doc). Exceptions only with comment + Exception index.
- **All form-like controls**: Any control that is part of the search/filter UX (e.g. date inputs in StatisticsHeader) must be inside a root that has the wrapper class, or must use the same `var(--sf-control-height)` etc. so size is consistent across screens.

## Width by role

- **Same role, same min/max**: Field **width** (min-width, max-width) is defined **by role** in `docs/design/search-fields-by-screen.md` (e.g. date/single-select, user block, extra block). The same role must use the **same** min/max on all screens; use `var(--sf-field-*-min)`, `var(--sf-field-*-max)` from `search-filter-standard.css` where defined, or the numeric values from the design doc. Do not use different max-width per screen (e.g. 220px on activity-log and 180px on statistics for the same role).
- **Shared blocks**: For blocks used on multiple screens (e.g. UserContextFilterBlock), use **block-level width** (`var(--sf-field-user-block-min)`, `var(--sf-field-user-block-max)`) or the same grid/field sizing on every screen so the block does not stretch differently and field widths stay comparable.
- **Aligning multiple screens**: When the requirement aligns search/filter UI **across two or more screens** (for example activity log and statistics), the **user block** (department, user name, user ID) must have the **same min/max width and visual size** on all aligned screens. Do not put the user block and another control (for example log type) in a **single** `1fr` cell. Give the user block its own column or sufficient min-width so it is not squeezed. Ref: `docs/workflow/ANALYSIS-user-field-size-activity-log-vs-statistics.md`.

## Same-name, different-context fields

If a field exists on **multiple screens with different semantics** (for example **startDate** / **endDate** on main search vs activity log), do **not** change or unify the definition unilaterally. Follow `docs/design/search-fields-by-screen.md` § "Same-name, different-semantics field - request feedback": **ask the user** how to proceed (unify as one definition vs keep per-screen), then apply the decision.

## When standard is undefined or ambiguous

Before implementing, check that the following are defined and clear: **common layout**, **search/filter field placement**, **input/select/button size** (wrapper + vars), **width by role**, **label/placeholder/button order**. If any is missing or ambiguous, **do not implement**. Respond with: (1) list of undefined/ambiguous items, (2) why each is needed, (3) request to define the standard first and then proceed, (4) optional recommended standard draft. Full checklist and response format: `docs/design/ux-frontend-standard-principles.md` §2.

## Required workflow before implementation

1. Classify the request by **role** (e.g. date, user-context block, extra block, action buttons).
2. Check that a **common standard exists** for that role (search-fields-by-screen.md, search-filter-standard.css, forms-and-filters.md).
3. If yes → apply the standard; do not re-define in component CSS.
4. If no or unclear → inform the user per `ux-frontend-standard-principles.md` §2; do not implement until the standard is defined or the user confirms.

## Prohibited

- Re-defining **height, padding, border, border-radius** for `.form-control` or filter buttons in component or shared block CSS when they are inside the standard wrapper.
- Using **different min/max width** for the same role on different screens (e.g. 220px on one, 180px on another for date/single-select).
- **Hardcoding** `height`, `padding`, `gap`, `min-width`, `max-width` in component CSS when the value exists in the standard; use `var(--sf-*)` or the wrapper.
- **Implementing** without checking the standard or without user feedback when the standard is unclear.

## Who uses this

- **UX**: When reviewing or designing search/filter UI, cite the above docs and verify designs match the field tables and single-application-point rule. If a standard is missing, request definition first per `ux-frontend-standard-principles.md`.
- **Frontend**: (1) Add the standard wrapper class to the form/filter root so control sizing comes from the standard file only. (2) Do not re-declare control height/padding in component or shared block CSS. (3) Apply width by role from search-fields-by-screen.md (same role = same min/max). (4) If the standard is undefined for the task, inform the user and do not implement until the standard is defined or confirmed.
